Assignment: Enterprise-grade Application Deployment and Hardening on Kubernetes
Project Title: Deploying a Secure Multi-Tier Application Using Helm on Kubernetes
Objective
Gain hands-on Kubernetes experience by deploying a prebuilt, multi-tier application using Helm and applying best practices in configuration management, security, and persistence.

Provided Stack
Use the following prebuilt container images:

nginx (frontend)

redis (in-memory cache)

postgres (persistent database)

A basic starter Helm chart structure and values.yaml will be provided.

Assignment Tasks
1. Helm Chart Packaging
Deploy the full stack using a single Helm chart with three subcharts (nginx, redis, postgres).

Use values.yaml to configure image names, ports, and replica counts.

2. Configuration Management
Create a ConfigMap to provide a custom nginx.conf.

Create a Secret to store PostgreSQL credentials (POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, POSTGRES_DB).

3. Probes and Health Checks
Add Readiness and Liveness Probes to:

nginx: check HTTP / returns 200

redis: TCP check on port 6379

postgres: TCP check on port 5432

4. Network Policies
Create a NetworkPolicy that:

Allows traffic from nginx to redis and postgres

Blocks all other pod-to-pod traffic by default

5. RBAC Configuration
Create a dedicated ServiceAccount for the Helm release.

Bind only the necessary permissions via a Role and RoleBinding (e.g., permissions to get, list, create pods and services in that namespace).

6. Persistent Volumes
Deploy postgres using a StatefulSet with a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC).

Use hostPath or dynamic provisioning depending on the cluster setup.

7. Backup Simulation
Create a Kubernetes CronJob that simulates a backup job:

Mounts the same volume as the database

Writes a dummy backup file like backup-<timestamp>.sql to /mnt/backups

Deliverables
A GitHub repository containing:

Helm chart with subcharts

YAMLs for ConfigMaps, Secrets, PVCs, RBAC, Probes, NetworkPolicies, and CronJob

